WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.240
(quiet music)
- Hi, my name is

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:02.073
Maggie McGrath.

00:00:02.073 --> 00:00:03.160
I'm the Editor of "ForbesWomen,"

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:04.180
and today I'm here to tell you

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:07.190
about our 16th "Annual
Power Women Ranking."

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:10.240
It's our list of the world's
100 most powerful women

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.600
across business, media, entertainment,

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.100
technology, finance, and politics.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.120
To report out our list of the
world's most powerful women,

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.980
the "ForbesWomen" team
combs through all the lists

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:22.737
that already exist here at "Forbes."

00:00:22.737 --> 00:00:25.457
"The Forbes 400," "The
World's Billionaires" lists,

00:00:25.457 --> 00:00:26.670
"Self-Made Women."

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:29.050
We have 13 billionaires on
this year's list, in fact.

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:30.870
We also look at the "Celeb 100,"

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:33.720
our list of the world's
highest paid entertainers.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:36.470
Some of those women, like
Taylor Swift and Rihanna,

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:39.670
have made our "Power Women"
list because they influence fans

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:42.100
around the globe through their
music, their social media,

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.790
and in the case of Rihanna
with Fenty, their business.

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:46.860
Newcomers in the media
and entertainment space

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.850
also include Reese
Witherspoon and Ava Duvernay.

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:51.920
Both women have production companies

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.840
and are shaping the way
Americans consume stories,

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.170
particularly stories about
underrepresented communities,

00:00:57.170 --> 00:00:59.300
about and by underrepresented communities,

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.770
which is hugely important,
as we head into 2020.

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:03.390
In prior years, when we've done

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:04.940
the "World's Most Powerful Women" list,

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:07.770
we've had leaders in South
America make the cover

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:09.030
of "Forbes" magazine.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:11.710
Political unrest in the
region has led to a drop-off

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:14.010
of nearly all of those figures,

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:16.750
but in business this year,
there's been a resurgence.

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:20.430
We have on this year's list
making her debut Paula Santilli.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.830
She is the head of PepsiCo Latin America,

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.030
a $7.4 billion business,

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:27.580
and we also have the new CFO of Petrobras,

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:29.830
one of the world's largest oil companies.

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:31.730
Both of these women were
appointed to their positions

00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:34.270
in the spring and make their
debut on the list this year

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:36.300
and give Latin America a resurgence

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.240
on our "Power Women" list.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:40.780
In Africa, we looked at heads of states

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:42.955
across every country in the continent.

00:01:42.955 --> 00:01:47.110
What's hard is those GDPs don't measure up

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:49.340
to some of the other GDPs in the world,

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:53.670
so these women who also aren't
always popularly elected

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:56.600
have a little bit of a
harder time making the list.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:59.480
We have the head of Ethiopia
on this year's list,

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:01.070
but a number of other ministers

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:03.360
of tourism and finance and economy

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:04.910
who are on our "Women to Watch" list,

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:06.500
which is a separate list that we have

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.470
as a web-only piece this year.

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:10.970
Coming in at number one
for the ninth year in a row

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:13.220
is German Chancellor Angela Merkel.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:17.260
She runs what is Europe's
largest GDP, largest economy.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:20.740
She has a huge impact on
the region and on the world.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:23.040
She sits on the European Council,

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.970
which appoints women
like Christine Lagarde

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:27.780
who is number two on this year's list.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.650
She runs the European Central Bank,

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:32.140
was appointed to this
position just this year

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:34.040
after running the
International Monetary Fund

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:35.300
for a number of years.

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:36.980
And Merkel also helps appoint women

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:38.490
like Ursula von der Leyen.

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:40.710
She is the new President
of the European Commission,

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:42.450
the EU's legislative arm,

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.544
and with that power, affects legislation

00:02:44.544 --> 00:02:47.340
that hits 700 million people

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:50.110
and brings her to number four on the list.

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:52.830
The highest ranking American
woman is Nancy Pelosi.

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:54.360
She's number three on the list

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:57.040
and the US Speaker of the
House for the second time

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.070
in recent history.

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:01.920
She served as Speaker from 2007 to 2011

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.080
and was on the "Power List" back then.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:07.490
Dropped off after she fell out of power

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:09.340
after the Democrats fell out of power,

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:11.370
but she is back on the list.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:14.170
And she is second in
line for the Presidency,

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:16.460
is the highest ranking elected woman

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:18.327
in the United States right now

00:03:18.327 --> 00:03:21.660
and leading impeachment
proceedings against the President.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:25.290
So, one could say that her
influence at this moment in time

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.670
is higher than it's ever been before.

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:29.860
So, it's a really strong
return to the list

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:31.390
for Speaker Pelosi.

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:33.890
Elsewhere in the top ten,
we have Melinda Gates,

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:36.410
who is the Chair of the Bill
and Melinda Gates Foundation

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:38.290
and affecting huge amounts of philanthropy

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:40.730
with their work abroad and in the US,

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:43.322
focusing on girl's education
and health and wellness.

00:03:43.322 --> 00:03:47.100
Some notable movements
include Queen Elizabeth.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:48.770
I talked to scholars and economists

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:51.490
about whether or not the
Queen is actually powerful,

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:54.810
and opinions range, let me tell you.

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.280
Last year, the Queen was
number 23 on the list.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:59.550
This year, she's number 40.

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:02.310
I think this is reflected
by dint of her giving

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:04.170
some of her patronages and charities

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:07.010
to her sons and her daughters-in-law.

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:09.900
We still think the Queen
is a historic figure

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:13.100
in the world and in politics
and a very important lady,

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:16.180
but as she is 93, it does appear

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:18.690
like she's ready to
transition some of her powers

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:21.430
to the King in Waiting, Prince Charles.

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:23.230
Another notable newcomer this year

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:26.960
is Greta Thunberg, the Swedish
climate change activist

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:30.450
who is just 16, spoke at the
UN General Assembly this year

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:33.890
and sparked millions of people
to pour into the streets

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:37.000
to protest against
inaction on climate change.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:39.140
She is number 100 on this year's list.

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:42.230
Our methodology does prioritize
assets under management,

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:46.010
company, market cap, revenue, country GDP.

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:47.450
Greta has none of these things,

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:50.870
but what she does have is
social media and influence.

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:53.570
She had more news clips about her

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:55.460
than almost any other woman on the list,

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:58.360
other than Angela Merkel and Nancy Pelosi.

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:02.310
So, she is a newcomer
and showing how power

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:05.200
can go beyond just money and market cap.

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:08.530
2017 was the year of Me Too and Time's Up,

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:12.080
and 2018 seemed to me the
year of fallout from that,

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:13.840
a lot of grappling with the revelations

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:15.380
that had occurred the year before.

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:18.490
2019 from my reporting seems
to be the year of action,

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:22.590
albeit slow action, maybe
shifts is the better word,

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:24.730
but you have the appointment of women

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:27.160
like Christine Lagarde
and Ursula von der Leyen.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:31.060
You see funds like All Raise
trying to get more money

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:33.310
in the hands of female entrepreneurs.

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:35.100
Change is happening.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:37.840
It's happening slowly, but
the women on the "Power List"

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:41.330
are using their power to
lift up the next generation

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:43.573
and to create gender parity for all.

